<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apelles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apelles was the most celebrated painter of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, active in the 4th century BCE. Born at Colophon, he trained at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> and in the painting school of Sicyon and became court painter to Philip II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. None of his works survive, but ancient writers praised his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a> rising from the sea and his portraits of Alexander.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apelles.jpg" alt="Apelles" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Antonio Guardi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_and_Campaspe_in_Apelles%27_studio_(c.1745),_by_Giovanni_Antonio_Guardi_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Much of what is known of Apelles comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>, who rated him superior to earlier and later painters and dated him to the 112th Olympiad (332–329 BCE), possibly because he painted a portrait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Apelles first studied under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephorus">Ephorus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> and, after gaining some celebrity, under Pamphilus at Sicyon, so that he is said to have combined Dorian thoroughness with Ionic grace. He was drawn to the court of Philip II, painted him and the young Alexander, and became the recognised court painter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. His picture of Alexander holding a thunderbolt was ranked by many beside the sculptor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysippus">Lysippus</a>'s Alexander with the spear. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, centuries later, found it unconvincing for making Alexander's complexion too dark.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>None of his paintings survives. Those recorded include Aphrodite Anadyomene, Aphrodite rising from the sea, for which tradition says Alexander's former mistress Campaspe was the model; a portrait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus I Monophthalmus</a> on horseback, shown in three-quarter view so as to hide the king's blind eye; and an allegory of Calumny, described by Lucian, in which an innocent youth is falsely accused by Ignorance, Envy, Treachery and Deceit. Pliny says the Calumny arose from a rival's false accusation that Apelles had joined a conspiracy against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV Philopator</a>, which almost cost him his life. Some pictures were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and displayed in public; the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> had Alexander's face in two of them replaced with that of Augustus.</p><p>Pliny attaches several sayings to him, perhaps from a lost treatise. Among them are his judgement that Protogenes knew when to take his hand from the picture, that a shoemaker should venture no further than the shoe, and Nulla dies sine linea, not a day without a line. Another story tells of a contest of ever finer lines drawn between Apelles and Protogenes on a panel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>. Pliny also says Apelles had a secret black varnish, which preserved paintings and softened their colour, and which was lost with his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Apelles is said to have been at work on an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a> of Kos when he died, and the picture was left unfinished, since no one had the skill to complete it. His fame lasted: Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> painters repeated his subjects. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandro-botticelli">Sandro Botticelli</a> based his Calumny of Apelles on Lucian's description and also his Birth of Venus on the painter's works, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> may have portrayed himself as Apelles in The School of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. The 15th-century mathematician Luca Pacioli wrote that even Apelles must now give way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Apelles” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It became famous for bronze sculpture, especially Lysippus, and for its school of painting, where <b>Apelles</b> studied.</blockquote><a href="/state/sicyon">Sicyon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Colophon, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Kos. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.12,27.14&amp;year=-335" aria-label="Colophon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5226 794 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Apelles"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5676 1064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5676 1064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.12,27.14&amp;year=-335">What was here in 335 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
